The method add(int index, E element). It checks position index, then if index == size (i.e., appending at end) linkLast(element); else linkBefore(element, node(index)). The comment: 

"@@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then for every valid index from the specified index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null if the old element at this position was null, or it equals the old element at this position."

Interpretation: For each i such that index <= i < oldSize (old size before insertion), after insertion, the element at position i+1 (next position) equals old element at position i, unless old element at i was null, in which case new element at i+1 is null (i.e., same). So basically shift right.

Thus spec: For all int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) . But need to handle null: if old element is null then new element is null, which .equals would cause NullPointerException. So we can use ( \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i+1) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))) ).

We also need premise that index is between 0 and old size inclusive. The method already ensures index valid; but spec should include condition: 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size()) => ... . However comment says "If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then ...". So we assert that condition implies shift property.

Thus final assert:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i+1) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));

Make sure to use this.get(i) method. Also note that after insertion size increased by 1. So i+1 is valid up to oldSize. Since new size = oldSize+1, i+1 <= oldSize. That's fine.

